! -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
! Is Phi a flux function in the divertor, and does the departure move density?
!
! Run:   jorek2_postproc < postproc_flux_function.in
! Then:  util/analyse_flux_function.py exprs_Rmin*.h5
!        util/analyse_flux_function.py nothermo/exprs_*.h5 thermo/exprs_*.h5   (A/B)
!
! WHY POSTPROC AND NOT VISIT. The quantity wanted is
!
!     v_psi = (v_ExB . grad psi) / |grad psi|
!
! which is IDENTICALLY ZERO if and only if Phi = Phi(psi). Computing it in VisIt
! means calling gradient() on the VTK export, and that export carries LINEAR
! cells while JOREK's elements are bicubic Bezier -- so the gradient is that of
! the linear interpolant, with cell-scale noise worst exactly where the mesh is
! coarse. postproc evaluates the derivatives analytically from the Bezier basis.
! For a diagnostic whose whole content is "are these two gradients parallel",
! that difference is not cosmetic.
!
! It also returns NUMBERS rather than pixels. The open question is not what the
! map looks like -- it is whether the radial ExB flux is a significant fraction
! of the parallel flux in the PFR, and that is a ratio, not a picture.
!
! WHAT IS EXPORTED AND WHY. There is no v_psi expression in JOREK, and
! V_ExB_pol is NOT it: that one is -(grad u . grad psi)/B_theta, the component
! of v_ExB ALONG the poloidal field. What is wanted is the CROSS product, i.e.
! the component across flux surfaces. It is reconstructed in the Python script
! from quantities that DO exist, with no numerical differentiation at all:
!
!     grad psi = ( -R*BZ, R*BR )        (from B_pol = (psi_Z, -psi_R)/R)
!     |grad psi| = R * Btheta
!  => v_psi = ( V_ExB_Z*BR - V_ExB_R*BZ ) / Btheta
!
! The R factors cancel exactly. vpar, ne, B_abs and Btheta are exported so the
! script can form the comparison that actually decides the question,
!
!     Gamma_psi / Gamma_par,pol = n*v_psi / ( n*vpar*Btheta/B )
!
! both being poloidal-plane flux densities, hence directly comparable.
!
! A_3 IS THE TRUE psi; Psi_N IS NOT MONOTONIC. get_psi_n (models/equil_info.f90:1011)
! REFLECTS the normalised flux inside the private region,
!     psi_n -> 2*psi_n_xpoint - psi_n     for psi_n < psi_n_xpoint and Z < Z_xpoint,
! so that PFR surfaces come out above the separatrix value like the SOL ones. A useful
! labelling, but it means grad(Psi_N) is ANTI-parallel to grad(psi) inside the PFR,
! parallel outside, and meaningless at the seam -- so Psi_N cannot be used to verify the
! orientation of grad psi. A_3 is psi itself ("Tor. Comp. of Magn. Vect. Pot. Multiplied
! by R (==Psi)") and is monotonic, so the script checks against that. Psi_N is still
! exported, for the separatrix contour and for masking.
!
! ORDER MATTERS, NAMES DO NOT. write_hdf5_2d stores the variable names into a
! character(len=1) array (mod_diag_output.f90:552, flagged in the source with a
! row of #), so every name in the file is truncated to its FIRST LETTER. The
! reader therefore keys on the ORDER of the expressions below. If you edit this
! list, pass the new list to the script with --vars.
! -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

namelist input

si-units             ! NOT "set units 0" -- that is not a command and is silently ignored

for step 1180 to 1180 do

  ! Order is load-bearing -- see the note above.
  expressions R Z Psi_N A_3 Phi ne T_e T_i vpar V_ExB_R V_ExB_Z BR BZ Btheta B_abs

  ! The divertor region: both legs, the X-point and the whole PFR between them.
  ! For AUG 38773 the strike points sit at (1.256,-1.055) and (1.593,-1.150),
  ! with the X-point near (1.43,-0.93). 300 x 300 is ~1.5 mm resolution, fine
  ! enough for the PFR cell (which spans ~R 1.30-1.50) without being slow.
  rectangle 1.15 1.72 300 -1.18 -0.78 300 0.

done
